   "id": "section-2-elementary-singular-signal-transform-encyclopedia",
   "title": "Section 2: Elementary & Singular Signal Transform Encyclopedia",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.1 The Dirac Delta & Delayed Impulse"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\delta(t) \\stackrel{\\mathcal{F}}{\\longleftrightarrow} 1"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\delta(t - t_0) \\stackrel{\\mathcal{F}}{\\longleftrightarrow} e^{-j\\omega t_0} = e^{-j 2\\pi f t_0}"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "*Proof:*"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "X(\\omega) = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty \\delta(t - t_0) e^{-j\\omega t} dt = e^{-j\\omega t_0} \\quad \\text{(via sifting property)}"
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.2 DC Signal ($1$) & Complex Exponential ($e^{j\\omega_0 t}$)"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "1 \\stackrel{\\mathcal{F}}{\\longleftrightarrow} 2\\pi \\delta(\\omega) = \\delta(f)"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "e^{j\\omega_0 t} \\stackrel{\\mathcal{F}}{\\longleftrightarrow} 2\\pi \\delta(\\omega - \\omega_0) = \\delta(f - f_0)"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "e^{-j\\omega_0 t} \\stackrel{\\mathcal{F}}{\\longleftrightarrow} 2\\pi \\delta(\\omega + \\omega_0) = \\delta(f + f_0)"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "*Proof via Inverse Synthesis:*"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty [2\\pi \\delta(\\omega - \\omega_0)] e^{j\\omega t} d\\omega = e^{j\\omega_0 t} \\quad \\blacksquare"
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.3 Sinusoids: $\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$ and $\\sin(\\omega_0 t)$"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Using Euler's identity $\\cos(\\omega_0 t) = \\frac{e^{j\\omega_0 t} + e^{-j\\omega_0 t}}{2}$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\boxed{\\cos(\\omega_0 t) \\stackrel{\\mathcal{F}}{\\longleftrightarrow} \\pi [\\delta(\\omega - \\omega_0) + \\delta(\\omega + \\omega_0)] = \\frac{1}{2}[\\delta(f - f_0) + \\delta(f + f_0)]}"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Using Euler's identity $\\sin(\\omega_0 t) = \\frac{e^{j\\omega_0 t} - e^{-j\\omega_0 t}}{2j}$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\boxed{\\sin(\\omega_0 t) \\stackrel{\\mathcal{F}}{\\longleftrightarrow} \\frac{\\pi}{j} [\\delta(\\omega - \\omega_0) - \\delta(\\omega + \\omega_0)] = \\frac{1}{2j}[\\delta(f - f_0) - \\delta(f + f_0)]}"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "In polar form for $\\sin(\\omega_0 t)$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\frac{\\pi}{j}\\delta(\\omega - \\omega_0) = -j\\pi \\delta(\\omega - \\omega_0) = \\pi e^{-j\\pi/2} \\delta(\\omega - \\omega_0)"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "-\\frac{\\pi}{j}\\delta(\\omega + \\omega_0) = +j\\pi \\delta(\\omega + \\omega_0) = \\pi e^{+j\\pi/2} \\delta(\\omega + \\omega_0)"
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.4 The Signum Function $\\operatorname{sgn}(t)$"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\operatorname{sgn}(t) = \\begin{cases} +1, & t > 0 \\\\ 0, & t = 0 \\\\ -1, & t < 0 \\end{cases}"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "The signum function is odd, so its transform must be purely imaginary and odd.\nRepresenting $\\operatorname{sgn}(t)$ as the limit of two decaying exponentials:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\operatorname{sgn}(t) = \\lim_{a \\to 0} \\left[ e^{-at}u(t) - e^{at}u(-t) \\right], \\quad a > 0"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\mathcal{F}\\{\\operatorname{sgn}(t)\\} = \\lim_{a \\to 0} \\left[ \\frac{1}{a + j\\omega} - \\frac{1}{a - j\\omega} \\right] = \\lim_{a \\to 0} \\left[ \\frac{-2j\\omega}{a^2 + \\omega^2} \\right] = \\frac{-2j\\omega}{\\omega^2} = \\frac{2}{j\\omega}"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\boxed{\\operatorname{sgn}(t) \\stackrel{\\mathcal{F}}{\\longleftrightarrow} \\frac{2}{j\\omega} = \\frac{1}{j\\pi f}}"
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.5 The Unit Step Function $u(t)$ & The DC Impulse Proof"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "The unit step can be decomposed uniquely into its even and odd parts:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "u(t) = u_e(t) + u_o(t) = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sgn}(t)"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Applying linearity:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\mathcal{F}\\{u(t)\\} = \\frac{1}{2}\\mathcal{F}\\{1\\} + \\frac{1}{2}\\mathcal{F}\\{\\operatorname{sgn}(t)\\} = \\frac{1}{2}[2\\pi\\delta(\\omega)] + \\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\frac{2}{j\\omega}\\right]"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\boxed{u(t) \\stackrel{\\mathcal{F}}{\\longleftrightarrow} \\frac{1}{j\\omega} + \\pi \\delta(\\omega) = \\frac{1}{j 2\\pi f} + \\frac{1}{2}\\delta(f)}"
    },
    {
     "t": "alert",
     "type": "CAUTION",
     "title": null,
     "text": "**Why $\\lim_{a\\to 0} e^{-at}u(t) = \\frac{1}{j\\omega}$ is FALSE:**  \nA classic trap is writing $\\mathcal{F}\\{u(t)\\} = \\lim_{a \\to 0}\\frac{1}{a+j\\omega} = \\frac{1}{j\\omega}$. This fails because the limit is taken pointwise on ordinary functions. In the theory of distributions (generalized functions), the Plemelj-Sokhotski theorem establishes:\n$$\\lim_{a \\to 0^+} \\frac{1}{a + j\\omega} = \\text{P.V.}\\left(\\frac{1}{j\\omega}\\right) + \\pi \\delta(\\omega)$$\nThe DC impulse $\\pi\\delta(\\omega)$ originates from the non-zero average area of $u(t)$ ($\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty u(t)dt = \\infty$, mean value $1/2$). Omitting it results in fatal errors in system integration!"
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.6 One-Sided Real Decaying Exponential ($e^{-at}u(t)$)"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "For $a > 0$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "x(t) = e^{-at}u(t) \\stackrel{\\mathcal{F}}{\\longleftrightarrow} X(\\omega) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-at} e^{-j\\omega t} dt = \\left. \\frac{e^{-(a+j\\omega)t}}{-(a+j\\omega)} \\right|_0^\\infty = \\frac{1}{a + j\\omega}"
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "**Magnitude Spectrum:** $|X(\\omega)| = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a^2 + \\omega^2}}$ (Even function of $\\omega$, maximum value $1/a$ at $\\omega = 0$).",
      "**Phase Spectrum:** $\\angle X(\\omega) = -\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\omega}{a}\\right)$ (Odd function of $\\omega$).",
      "**3-dB Cutoff Frequency:** $\\omega_{3\\text{dB}} = a\\text{ rad/s} \\iff f_{3\\text{dB}} = \\frac{a}{2\\pi}\\text{ Hz}$, where $|X(\\omega_{3\\text{dB}})| = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|X(0)|$."
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "For negative-time exponential:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "e^{at}u(-t) \\stackrel{\\mathcal{F}}{\\longleftrightarrow} \\frac{1}{a - j\\omega} \\quad (a > 0)"
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.7 Two-Sided Symmetric Exponential ($e^{-a|t|}$)"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "For $a > 0$, $x(t) = e^{-a|t|} = e^{-at}u(t) + e^{at}u(-t)$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "X(\\omega) = \\frac{1}{a + j\\omega} + \\frac{1}{a - j\\omega} = \\frac{(a - j\\omega) + (a + j\\omega)}{a^2 + \\omega^2} = \\frac{2a}{a^2 + \\omega^2}"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\boxed{e^{-a|t|} \\stackrel{\\mathcal{F}}{\\longleftrightarrow} \\frac{2a}{a^2 + \\omega^2} = \\frac{2a}{a^2 + 4\\pi^2 f^2} \\quad (a > 0)}"
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "$x(t)$ is Real and Even $\\implies X(\\omega)$ is purely Real and Even.",
      "Area under $x(t)$: $\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{-a|t|}dt = X(0) = \\frac{2a}{a^2} = \\frac{2}{a}$."
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.8 The Gaussian Pulse ($e^{-at^2}$) & Self-Reciprocity"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "For $a > 0$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "x(t) = e^{-at^2} \\stackrel{\\mathcal{F}}{\\longleftrightarrow} X(\\omega) = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{a}} e^{-\\frac{\\omega^2}{4a}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{a}} e^{-\\frac{\\pi^2 f^2}{a}}"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "*Self-Reciprocity Theorem:*\nWhen $a = \\pi$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "x(t) = e^{-\\pi t^2} \\stackrel{\\mathcal{F}}{\\longleftrightarrow} X(f) = e^{-\\pi f^2}"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "The Gaussian function is an **eigenfunction** of the continuous Fourier transform with eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1$."
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "*Uncertainty Principle Bound:*\nDefining the root-mean-square effective duration $\\Delta t$ and bandwidth $\\Delta \\omega$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\Delta t = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4a}}, \\quad \\Delta \\omega = \\sqrt{a} \\implies \\Delta t \\cdot \\Delta \\omega = \\frac{1}{2}"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "The Gaussian pulse strictly achieves the **minimum possible time-bandwidth product** ($\\Delta t \\cdot \\Delta \\omega = 1/2$) mandated by the Heisenberg-Gabor uncertainty relation."
    }
   ]
